WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.444
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:12.800 --> 00:00:16.320
HANSMAN: The invention of the
metric system in 1790 improved

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:19.060
matters somewhat by
basing units of measure

00:00:19.060 --> 00:00:21.840
on more widely agreed
upon standards,

00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:25.350
like the circumference
of the Earth.

00:00:25.350 --> 00:00:27.270
Back at the turn
of this century,

00:00:27.270 --> 00:00:29.970
Lord Kelvin, namesake of the
international temperature

00:00:29.970 --> 00:00:32.100
standard, said it best.

00:00:32.100 --> 00:00:35.190
One has but a poor understanding
of that which one cannot

00:00:35.190 --> 00:00:36.830
measure.

00:00:36.830 --> 00:00:40.970
Today we try to have rational,
simple, and consistent

00:00:40.970 --> 00:00:45.530
measurement systems based on
sound scientific principles.

00:00:45.530 --> 00:00:48.320
The goal of this video
series is to introduce you

00:00:48.320 --> 00:00:50.420
to the principles
of measurement.

00:00:50.420 --> 00:00:53.200
But we'll also show you some
of the standard techniques

00:00:53.200 --> 00:00:55.180
and equipment.

00:00:55.180 --> 00:00:57.730
The series is designed
as a basic introduction

00:00:57.730 --> 00:00:58.840
to measurement.

00:00:58.840 --> 00:01:00.940
It should help you
whether you are attempting

00:01:00.940 --> 00:01:04.599
to learn about measurement
as training for new job,

00:01:04.599 --> 00:01:08.980
or you are trying to solve a
particular measurement problem.

00:01:08.980 --> 00:01:11.200
The series may not give
you all the answers.

00:01:11.200 --> 00:01:13.240
But after watching,
I hope that you

00:01:13.240 --> 00:01:15.680
will at least be able to
ask the right questions.

00:01:29.126 --> 00:01:32.612
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:02:29.400 --> 00:02:31.620
I'm sitting up in the
summit of Mount Washington.

00:02:31.620 --> 00:02:35.070
And the actual summit is located
underneath this reference data

00:02:35.070 --> 00:02:38.460
marker placed here by
the US Geological Survey.

00:02:38.460 --> 00:02:40.990
We're going to talk about
techniques to measure distance,

00:02:40.990 --> 00:02:44.820
velocity, and acceleration
using techniques as simple

00:02:44.820 --> 00:02:47.310
as something like
this ruler, and as

00:02:47.310 --> 00:02:51.850
sophisticated as this global
positioning system unit.

00:02:51.850 --> 00:02:53.910
The fundamental standards
for the measurement

00:02:53.910 --> 00:02:56.400
of distance, velocity,
and acceleration

00:02:56.400 --> 00:02:58.410
are length and time.

00:02:58.410 --> 00:03:01.170
I've come here to the museum
at the National Institute

00:03:01.170 --> 00:03:03.450
of Standards and
Technology to show

00:03:03.450 --> 00:03:07.020
you a few examples of current
and historical standards

00:03:07.020 --> 00:03:08.580
of length and time.

00:03:08.580 --> 00:03:13.140
The museum has a mission to
preserve the historic evolution

00:03:13.140 --> 00:03:15.190
of physical measurement.

00:03:15.190 --> 00:03:18.470
Now, the current standard
of time is the second.

00:03:18.470 --> 00:03:23.100
And it's defined in terms of the
atomic transitions of caesium

00:03:23.100 --> 00:03:24.720
atoms.

00:03:24.720 --> 00:03:28.410
Caesium atoms are part
of an atomic clock.

00:03:28.410 --> 00:03:32.730
And this is one of the
original cesium ovens.

00:03:32.730 --> 00:03:36.330
Cesium was heated,
and a cesium beam

00:03:36.330 --> 00:03:38.340
would be emitted from
the front of this oven

00:03:38.340 --> 00:03:40.320
into the atomic clock.

00:03:40.320 --> 00:03:43.830
Now, it's not practical to
have atomic clocks for day

00:03:43.830 --> 00:03:45.590
to day time measurement.

00:03:45.590 --> 00:03:48.210
And today we have very
good working standards

00:03:48.210 --> 00:03:50.910
in terms of quartz
crystal oscillators,

00:03:50.910 --> 00:03:55.388
which are seen in things
as common as my wristwatch.

00:03:55.388 --> 00:04:00.620
Now, the international
standard for length measurement

00:04:00.620 --> 00:04:01.910
is the meter.

00:04:01.910 --> 00:04:04.640
And the meter was established
as an international standard

00:04:04.640 --> 00:04:08.420
at the International Conference
of Weights and Measures

00:04:08.420 --> 00:04:10.910
in 1889.

00:04:10.910 --> 00:04:14.330
Because the US was a
member of that conference,

00:04:14.330 --> 00:04:20.480
it received one of the prototype
meters, which I have here.

00:04:20.480 --> 00:04:25.340
This prototype was one of 31
that was built at that time.

00:04:25.340 --> 00:04:29.960
And the one that was closest
to the existing meter

00:04:29.960 --> 00:04:33.140
was preserved in Paris as
the international standard.

00:04:33.140 --> 00:04:36.830
And each of the countries
that participated

00:04:36.830 --> 00:04:39.770
received at least one
of these meter bars.

00:04:39.770 --> 00:04:43.910
The US received number 27.

00:04:43.910 --> 00:04:47.660
This meter bar is built
out of platinum and iridium

00:04:47.660 --> 00:04:53.090
and is accurate to
within 1.6 microns

00:04:53.090 --> 00:04:54.920
at zero degrees centigrade.

00:04:54.920 --> 00:04:58.730
It's 1.6 microns
short of a true meter.

00:04:58.730 --> 00:05:06.500
Now, this was the US
standard from 1893 to 1960.

00:05:06.500 --> 00:05:09.650
And the current standard for
the measurement of length,

00:05:09.650 --> 00:05:12.830
or definition of
a meter is defined

00:05:12.830 --> 00:05:16.490
in terms of the speed of light.

00:05:16.490 --> 00:05:19.880
Some historical length
standards are based on people.

00:05:19.880 --> 00:05:23.000
For example, this is a
replica of an Egyptian royal

00:05:23.000 --> 00:05:26.310
qubit, which was
used about 1500 B.C.

00:05:26.310 --> 00:05:29.360
And it was based on the
length of the forearm

00:05:29.360 --> 00:05:31.220
of the pharaoh Amenhotep.

00:05:33.920 --> 00:05:37.280
At MIT we also have
a unit of length,

00:05:37.280 --> 00:05:39.070
which was based on a person.

00:05:39.070 --> 00:05:42.140
And we've arranged to talk
to Oliver Smoot about how

00:05:42.140 --> 00:05:43.620
this happened.

00:05:43.620 --> 00:05:47.270
I had the privilege to
meet with Oliver Smoot, who

00:05:47.270 --> 00:05:49.250
is a living length standard.

00:05:49.250 --> 00:05:51.680
And Ollie, this
honor is normally

00:05:51.680 --> 00:05:53.990
reserved for kings and pharaohs.

00:05:53.990 --> 00:05:55.310
How did it happen to you?

00:05:55.310 --> 00:05:56.809
SMOOT: Well I don't
know about them.

00:05:56.809 --> 00:05:58.500
But I got it because I'm short.

00:05:58.500 --> 00:05:59.690
HANSMAN: You're short.

00:05:59.690 --> 00:06:00.773
SMOOT: Well, I don't know.

00:06:00.773 --> 00:06:04.874
5'7" I guess is below
average these days.

00:06:04.874 --> 00:06:07.290
HANSMAN: How did that get you
to become a length standard?

00:06:07.290 --> 00:06:09.950
SMOOT: When I was
a freshman at MIT

00:06:09.950 --> 00:06:13.880
we lived in a fraternity house
on the Boston side, Lambda Chi

00:06:13.880 --> 00:06:15.020
Alpha.

00:06:15.020 --> 00:06:18.950
And to get to class you had
to go across the Mass Avenue

00:06:18.950 --> 00:06:22.160
Bridge, which is a long bridge.

00:06:22.160 --> 00:06:25.220
You know exactly, I'm sure.

00:06:25.220 --> 00:06:29.540
And our pledge master said,
he was late too often.

00:06:29.540 --> 00:06:34.490
So he wanted us within a
week to measure the bridge.

00:06:34.490 --> 00:06:37.580
And he made all
14 of the freshmen

00:06:37.580 --> 00:06:40.817
stand up, and looked
at us, and said, Smoot,

00:06:40.817 --> 00:06:41.650
you're the shortest.

00:06:41.650 --> 00:06:43.040
So we'll do it in Smoots.

00:06:43.040 --> 00:06:44.960
HANSMAN: Okay, and
how many Smoots was it

00:06:44.960 --> 00:06:46.220
across the bridge?

00:06:46.220 --> 00:06:50.450
SMOOT: 364.4 and an ear.

00:06:50.450 --> 00:06:52.490
HANSMAN: How did you
get the 0.4 and an ear?

00:06:52.490 --> 00:06:54.290
SMOOT: Well, actually there--

00:06:54.290 --> 00:06:56.630
when we got to the
last expansion joint,

00:06:56.630 --> 00:07:00.110
there's an actual end
to the bridge there.

00:07:00.110 --> 00:07:01.890
It came up to about my knees.

00:07:01.890 --> 00:07:04.250
So we did 0.4.

00:07:04.250 --> 00:07:07.820
And decided that
since we knew there

00:07:07.820 --> 00:07:11.330
were errors in the measure,
that we'd be honest engineers

00:07:11.330 --> 00:07:15.350
and put a Greek E, which
means error measurement.

00:07:15.350 --> 00:07:17.870
And then we decided
that most people

00:07:17.870 --> 00:07:19.120
wouldn't know what that meant.

00:07:19.120 --> 00:07:20.390
So we wrote an ear.

00:07:20.390 --> 00:07:24.080
Because maybe that's about
the right amount of error.

00:07:24.080 --> 00:07:27.050
HANSMAN: There's a little bridge
here at the plaza at NIST.

00:07:27.050 --> 00:07:29.270
I wonder if I could
talk you into making

00:07:29.270 --> 00:07:31.460
a little measurement of
this bridge in Smoots.

00:07:31.460 --> 00:07:34.220
SMOOT: Well, if it weren't for
MIT being involved in this,

00:07:34.220 --> 00:07:34.990
I don't think so.

00:07:34.990 --> 00:07:35.560
But sure.

00:07:35.560 --> 00:07:35.990
HANSMAN: All right.

00:07:35.990 --> 00:07:37.230
This will be a
credible measurement.

00:07:37.230 --> 00:07:39.540
Now, let's start at the edge
of the water, over here.

00:07:39.540 --> 00:07:40.210
SMOOT: Okay.

00:07:40.210 --> 00:07:42.130
HANSMAN: Okay.

00:07:42.130 --> 00:07:42.740
Okay, Ollie.

00:07:42.740 --> 00:07:44.323
I'm going to put a
reference mark here

00:07:44.323 --> 00:07:46.440
at the start of the
bridge over the water.

00:07:46.440 --> 00:07:48.170
So.

00:07:48.170 --> 00:07:50.594
SMOOT: All right.

00:07:50.594 --> 00:07:51.510
Let's see what we get.

00:07:51.510 --> 00:07:52.160
HANSMAN: Okay, yeah.

00:07:52.160 --> 00:07:52.970
Let's see what you did.

00:07:52.970 --> 00:07:53.469
All right?

00:07:55.790 --> 00:07:56.290
Okay.

00:07:56.290 --> 00:07:56.720
Now.

00:07:56.720 --> 00:07:57.450
SMOOT: Aim for the top.

00:07:57.450 --> 00:07:58.241
HANSMAN: All right.

00:07:58.241 --> 00:07:59.208
Your head's the top.

00:07:59.208 --> 00:07:59.708
SMOOT: Okay.

00:07:59.708 --> 00:08:00.291
HANSMAN: Okay.

00:08:00.291 --> 00:08:03.152
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:08:24.310 --> 00:08:24.910
Okay, Ollie.

00:08:24.910 --> 00:08:28.420
We've now officially measured
the bridge here at the National

00:08:28.420 --> 00:08:30.430
Institute of Standards
and Technology.

00:08:30.430 --> 00:08:34.580
And it looks like you're about
1/10 of a Smoot short of 4.

00:08:34.580 --> 00:08:35.350
SMOOT: Yeah.

00:08:35.350 --> 00:08:38.055
HANSMAN: So we're 3.9 Smoots.

00:08:38.055 --> 00:08:39.450
SMOOT: Plus or minus an ear.

00:08:39.450 --> 00:08:40.241
HANSMAN: All right.

00:08:40.241 --> 00:08:41.179
Plus or minus an ear.

00:08:41.179 --> 00:08:41.679
All right.

00:08:41.679 --> 00:08:44.990
Now, you said
you're about 5' 7".

00:08:44.990 --> 00:08:47.665
And so one Smoot is
about 5 feet 7 inches.

00:08:50.560 --> 00:08:51.860
You think you're still 5' 7"?

00:08:51.860 --> 00:08:55.591
Is the Smoot a stable
reference standard?

00:08:55.591 --> 00:09:00.220
SMOOT: Maybe, because
my hair is thinner.

00:09:00.220 --> 00:09:02.000
I don't know.

00:09:02.000 --> 00:09:03.760
Sometime, I guess
I'll get shorter.

00:09:03.760 --> 00:09:06.700
But so far, it seems
to be about the same.

00:09:06.700 --> 00:09:09.250
HANSMAN: I've come to one
of the experimental research

00:09:09.250 --> 00:09:11.890
facilities here at
MIT's Lincoln Laboratory

00:09:11.890 --> 00:09:14.440
to talk about distance,
velocity, and acceleration

00:09:14.440 --> 00:09:15.890
measurement.

00:09:15.890 --> 00:09:19.760
The antenna you see above me is
an air traffic control radar,

00:09:19.760 --> 00:09:21.700
which is used to
measure the position

00:09:21.700 --> 00:09:23.810
and velocity of aircraft.

00:09:23.810 --> 00:09:26.590
This is the radar
controller station

00:09:26.590 --> 00:09:30.670
at the Lincoln Laboratory
experimental radar facility.

00:09:30.670 --> 00:09:33.170
Perhaps the simplest method
for measuring distance

00:09:33.170 --> 00:09:35.800
is by the use of
calibrated length scales,

00:09:35.800 --> 00:09:39.460
such as this tape measure
or this ruler here.

00:09:39.460 --> 00:09:41.380
Now, the maximum
resolution you can

00:09:41.380 --> 00:09:45.190
get with a calibrated
scale, such as this ruler,

00:09:45.190 --> 00:09:49.210
depends on the minimum
graduation of the scale.

00:09:49.210 --> 00:09:53.330
You can see this ruler is
calibrated both in centimeters

00:09:53.330 --> 00:09:54.670
and in inches.

00:09:54.670 --> 00:09:57.330
And the finest graduation
of the centimeter scale

00:09:57.330 --> 00:09:59.120
is 1 millimeter.

00:09:59.120 --> 00:10:03.490
So this system is really
only usable for accuracies

00:10:03.490 --> 00:10:06.560
down to, say, a fraction
of a millimeter.

00:10:06.560 --> 00:10:09.940
Now, rulers and
tape measures are

00:10:09.940 --> 00:10:12.760
good for measuring
linear distances

00:10:12.760 --> 00:10:15.730
where you can actually put
the edge you want to measure

00:10:15.730 --> 00:10:17.100
right up against the scale.

00:10:17.100 --> 00:10:21.920
So you can get a fairly accurate
measurement of the dimensions.

00:10:21.920 --> 00:10:23.620
Now, you get into
trouble if you try

00:10:23.620 --> 00:10:25.210
to measure something
like the diameter

00:10:25.210 --> 00:10:27.460
of this pipe with a ruler.

00:10:27.460 --> 00:10:32.410
The problem is that, because you
can't put the edge of the pipe

00:10:32.410 --> 00:10:35.020
right up against the
ruler, you run into what

00:10:35.020 --> 00:10:36.850
we call parallax error.

00:10:36.850 --> 00:10:38.680
If you move your
head, what happens

00:10:38.680 --> 00:10:41.980
is that the apparent edge of
the pipe will move around.

00:10:41.980 --> 00:10:44.810
So you can't get a very
accurate measurement.

00:10:44.810 --> 00:10:48.010
Now, we can actually use
something like this caliper,

00:10:48.010 --> 00:10:51.410
here, to measure the
dimension of the pipe.

00:10:51.410 --> 00:10:52.480
What I can do--

00:10:52.480 --> 00:10:54.280
let me just do it
from the top, here--

00:10:54.280 --> 00:10:57.850
is squeeze down on the
pipe with this caliper.

00:10:57.850 --> 00:11:01.450
And you can see this is an
electronic caliper, which

00:11:01.450 --> 00:11:08.590
measures the diameter at
about 15.98 millimeters.

00:11:08.590 --> 00:11:10.990
I can also flip
the calipers over

00:11:10.990 --> 00:11:14.740
and use these feelers to
measure the inside diameter

00:11:14.740 --> 00:11:16.020
of the pipe.

00:11:16.020 --> 00:11:20.530
And the caliper also
has a probe here

00:11:20.530 --> 00:11:22.660
that can be used
for measuring down

00:11:22.660 --> 00:11:25.630
inside the pipe, the
depth of the pipe.

00:11:25.630 --> 00:11:30.660
Now, I could also use something
that we call a micrometer.

00:11:30.660 --> 00:11:34.020
This is very commonly used
in mechanical measurements,

00:11:34.020 --> 00:11:35.640
machining, things like that.

00:11:35.640 --> 00:11:39.270
And essentially, what it
is, is a calibrated screw.

00:11:39.270 --> 00:11:42.450
What I do is I
twist the screw down

00:11:42.450 --> 00:11:45.050
until it squeezes on the pipe.

00:11:45.050 --> 00:11:50.160
And when the screw hits the
pipe, it stops rotating.

00:11:50.160 --> 00:11:52.770
Now, because I have a fair
amount of mechanical advantage

00:11:52.770 --> 00:11:56.310
twisting the screw,
I need to be careful

00:11:56.310 --> 00:11:58.560
that I actually don't
deform the pipe-- squeeze it

00:11:58.560 --> 00:12:00.100
so much that I deform it.

00:12:00.100 --> 00:12:03.690
And the way I do that is by
using this little knob here,

00:12:03.690 --> 00:12:05.220
which is a spring-loaded knob.

00:12:05.220 --> 00:12:08.850
And essentially, what I do
is I rotate the screw using

00:12:08.850 --> 00:12:10.410
the spring-loaded knob.

00:12:10.410 --> 00:12:13.140
And it always applies the
same amount of torque.

00:12:13.140 --> 00:12:15.330
So I essentially get the
same tension on the screw

00:12:15.330 --> 00:12:16.415
every time.

00:12:16.415 --> 00:12:19.660
And it makes the
measurement repeatable.

00:12:19.660 --> 00:12:23.500
Now, it's common for machinists
to use something called a dial

00:12:23.500 --> 00:12:24.522
gauge here.

00:12:24.522 --> 00:12:26.230
And essentially, what
the dial gauge does

00:12:26.230 --> 00:12:29.410
is I have this plunger that
pushes up against the object.

00:12:29.410 --> 00:12:32.740
And it will rotate the dial.

00:12:32.740 --> 00:12:35.980
So there's a very fine
mechanism inside the gauge that

00:12:35.980 --> 00:12:39.160
will make a measurement
of the position

00:12:39.160 --> 00:12:40.950
and show it on
this needle, here.

00:12:40.950 --> 00:12:44.620
And you can see that just my
jiggling around here as I talk

00:12:44.620 --> 00:12:46.780
is causing the system to jump.

00:12:46.780 --> 00:12:49.450
Now, because the
mechanism is very fine,

00:12:49.450 --> 00:12:52.630
what happens is that
sometimes the needle zero

00:12:52.630 --> 00:12:54.230
position will shift.

00:12:54.230 --> 00:12:58.120
And you can see that I can
just rotate the dial, here,

00:12:58.120 --> 00:13:01.170
to re-zero the instrument.

00:13:01.170 --> 00:13:04.740
Now, for some applications,
you want an electrical output

00:13:04.740 --> 00:13:07.290
rather than a mechanical
output of position.

00:13:07.290 --> 00:13:09.900
And there are different
ways you can do this.

00:13:09.900 --> 00:13:12.870
One common technique is
by using a potentiometer.

00:13:12.870 --> 00:13:15.780
And they're are, in
fact, many potentiometers

00:13:15.780 --> 00:13:16.860
in this oscilloscope.

00:13:16.860 --> 00:13:19.590
All the knobs,
here, are connected

00:13:19.590 --> 00:13:23.610
to potentiometers, which are
essentially just resistors.

00:13:23.610 --> 00:13:26.400
And as I rotate the position
of the knob, what I'm doing

00:13:26.400 --> 00:13:30.840
is changing the value
of the resistance.

00:13:30.840 --> 00:13:33.120
So you can see, as I change
the position of the knob

00:13:33.120 --> 00:13:37.560
here, what I'm doing is changing
the resistance, which then

00:13:37.560 --> 00:13:39.585
shows up as a change
in voltage on the face

00:13:39.585 --> 00:13:40.720
of the oscilloscope, here.

00:13:40.720 --> 00:13:43.170
So if I change it
a lot, you ought

00:13:43.170 --> 00:13:46.020
to be able to see that
show up on the scope.

00:13:46.020 --> 00:13:50.130
Now, this is a measurement
of rotary position.

00:13:50.130 --> 00:13:54.370
But I also am interested,
sometimes, in linear position.

00:13:54.370 --> 00:13:56.730
I can also use a
potentiometer there.

00:13:56.730 --> 00:14:00.600
But we can use a technique
called a Linear Variable

00:14:00.600 --> 00:14:04.230
Displacement
Transducer, or LVDT.

00:14:04.230 --> 00:14:07.170
Now, what an LVDT
is, is essentially

00:14:07.170 --> 00:14:10.685
an inductor, which is in
this metal core, here.

00:14:10.685 --> 00:14:16.440
And inside the inductor
is a magnetic material--

00:14:16.440 --> 00:14:19.030
I think, this
case, it's nickel--

00:14:19.030 --> 00:14:21.270
which goes down
inside the inductor.

00:14:21.270 --> 00:14:24.570
When it does, it
changes the inductance,

00:14:24.570 --> 00:14:27.010
which is measured by a circuit.

00:14:27.010 --> 00:14:29.920
The circuit is powered by
the small power supply here

00:14:29.920 --> 00:14:32.880
and is actually
inside the metal tube.

00:14:32.880 --> 00:14:35.730
The output of the circuit
is a voltage, which I'm now

00:14:35.730 --> 00:14:37.410
showing on the oscilloscope.

00:14:37.410 --> 00:14:41.190
And you can see that if I change
the position of the plunger,

00:14:41.190 --> 00:14:43.770
I get an electrical
output of voltage.

00:14:43.770 --> 00:14:44.910
Okay.

00:14:44.910 --> 00:14:47.160
If I pull the core
too far out, you

00:14:47.160 --> 00:14:49.530
can see that the
instrument saturates.

00:14:49.530 --> 00:14:50.220
Okay.

00:14:50.220 --> 00:14:54.100
So its usable range is
just a few inches, here,

00:14:54.100 --> 00:14:56.710
in the middle of the motion.

00:14:56.710 --> 00:14:59.850
So far, the smallest distances
we've talked about measuring

00:14:59.850 --> 00:15:04.590
are a thousandth of an inch
or a fraction of a millimeter.

00:15:04.590 --> 00:15:07.450
Sometimes, you need to make
measurements more accurately.

00:15:07.450 --> 00:15:10.950
And to do this, you can't really
use mechanical techniques.

00:15:10.950 --> 00:15:13.960
However, you can use
optical techniques.

00:15:13.960 --> 00:15:19.230
For example, this laser here
has a-- you can see the red dot

00:15:19.230 --> 00:15:20.790
on my hand--

00:15:20.790 --> 00:15:24.690
has a wavelength
of 633 nanometers,

00:15:24.690 --> 00:15:28.830
which is about 0.6
millionths of a meter.

00:15:28.830 --> 00:15:30.960
And by using
interference techniques,

00:15:30.960 --> 00:15:35.050
we can make measurements
on that level of accuracy.

00:15:35.050 --> 00:15:38.160
Now, there are many different
types of interferometers.

00:15:38.160 --> 00:15:40.860
And what I want to do is talk
to you about a simple one,

00:15:40.860 --> 00:15:43.270
just to give you a
feel for the concept.

00:15:43.270 --> 00:15:46.140
This is an example of a
Michelson interferometer,

00:15:46.140 --> 00:15:50.760
which can accurately measure the
difference between two paths.

00:15:50.760 --> 00:15:54.660
Light comes from the laser and
is split at a beam splitter

00:15:54.660 --> 00:15:59.190
into a measurement path
and a reference path.

00:15:59.190 --> 00:16:03.900
The light goes out and is
reflected off of a mirror

00:16:03.900 --> 00:16:07.890
and sent back to the beam
splitter along both paths.

00:16:07.890 --> 00:16:10.620
The light is then
recombined and picked up

00:16:10.620 --> 00:16:14.100
in a detector, which measures
intensity of the combined

00:16:14.100 --> 00:16:15.390
light.

00:16:15.390 --> 00:16:19.320
Now, if the paths are
identical, the waves

00:16:19.320 --> 00:16:22.950
from the reference path
and the measurement path

00:16:22.950 --> 00:16:26.520
will be in phase and
will add together.

00:16:26.520 --> 00:16:33.160
However, if the paths are
different by a quarter

00:16:33.160 --> 00:16:35.290
of a wavelength,
what will happen

00:16:35.290 --> 00:16:38.400
is the waves will
actually cancel--

00:16:38.400 --> 00:16:40.840
will come back 180
degrees out of phase

00:16:40.840 --> 00:16:44.450
and cancel-- so there'll be
no intensity at the detector.

00:16:44.450 --> 00:16:48.880
So as the measurement
path increases

00:16:48.880 --> 00:16:52.780
in length, what will happen
is the intensity measured

00:16:52.780 --> 00:16:56.470
at the detector will go
through minimums and maximums.

00:16:56.470 --> 00:16:58.200
We call these fringes.

00:16:58.200 --> 00:17:00.520
And by counting the
number of fringes,

00:17:00.520 --> 00:17:04.329
you can actually measure
the change in length

00:17:04.329 --> 00:17:06.202
along the measurement path.

00:17:06.202 --> 00:17:07.660
And again, as you
can see, this can

00:17:07.660 --> 00:17:09.987
be accurate to a fraction
of a wavelength, which

00:17:09.987 --> 00:17:10.945
is very small distance.

00:17:13.520 --> 00:17:15.619
Many techniques for
measuring distance

00:17:15.619 --> 00:17:17.599
use the principle
of time of flight

00:17:17.599 --> 00:17:20.960
of some wave to make the
distance measurement.

00:17:20.960 --> 00:17:25.250
For example, this ultrasonic
distance measuring unit, here,

00:17:25.250 --> 00:17:29.210
is used for measuring
the dimensions of rooms.

00:17:29.210 --> 00:17:32.090
So when I push this
button, what happens

00:17:32.090 --> 00:17:36.890
is an ultrasonic wave is
emitted from this transducer,

00:17:36.890 --> 00:17:39.770
is sent out, and
bounced off the wall.

00:17:39.770 --> 00:17:43.760
The reflected wave is picked
up by this second transducer,

00:17:43.760 --> 00:17:46.370
over here.

00:17:46.370 --> 00:17:49.040
If I measure the
round trip time,

00:17:49.040 --> 00:17:51.740
I can figure out the
distance to the wall.

00:17:51.740 --> 00:17:54.770
The distance is essentially
the round trip time,

00:17:54.770 --> 00:17:59.240
times the speed of sound,
divided by 2 because it

00:17:59.240 --> 00:18:01.580
was the out and return time.

00:18:01.580 --> 00:18:05.150
Now, that measurement
will be fairly accurate

00:18:05.150 --> 00:18:07.440
if you know the speed
of sound in air.

00:18:07.440 --> 00:18:11.250
Now, the speed of sound is
about 300 meters per second.

00:18:11.250 --> 00:18:14.490
However, it depends
on the temperature.

00:18:14.490 --> 00:18:17.300
So this measurement
will only be accurate

00:18:17.300 --> 00:18:21.740
if you are at the temperature
that the unit is calibrated for

00:18:21.740 --> 00:18:26.190
or if you correct for changes
in the speed of sound.

00:18:26.190 --> 00:18:29.750
Now, we actually looked at
another ultrasonic measuring

00:18:29.750 --> 00:18:32.450
unit when we were down at
the Omega Engineering labs.

00:18:32.450 --> 00:18:33.980
And let's go look at that now.

00:18:33.980 --> 00:18:38.452
This is an ultrasonic distance
and level measurement system.

00:18:38.452 --> 00:18:39.910
And what happens
here is that there

00:18:39.910 --> 00:18:44.120
is an ultrasonic transducer
in the top of the unit, which

00:18:44.120 --> 00:18:47.510
transmits an acoustic
wave, which reflects off

00:18:47.510 --> 00:18:49.430
of the object we're
trying to measure

00:18:49.430 --> 00:18:52.520
and comes back
down into the unit.

00:18:52.520 --> 00:18:55.310
The transducer actually
looks something like this.

00:18:59.790 --> 00:19:03.200
As I move the plexiglass
plate, here, you

00:19:03.200 --> 00:19:04.800
can see that the
distance is shorter.

00:19:04.800 --> 00:19:07.490
So it takes less time for the
wave to go up to the plexiglass

00:19:07.490 --> 00:19:08.060
and back.

00:19:12.025 --> 00:19:13.400
Now, the beauty
of this method is

00:19:13.400 --> 00:19:15.140
that you don't
need direct contact

00:19:15.140 --> 00:19:17.420
between the sensor
and the object

00:19:17.420 --> 00:19:19.100
you're trying to measure.

00:19:19.100 --> 00:19:21.020
Acoustic time-of-flight
techniques

00:19:21.020 --> 00:19:23.480
can also be used for making
distance measurements

00:19:23.480 --> 00:19:26.240
in fluids, such as water.

00:19:26.240 --> 00:19:28.920
Now, the speed of sound
in seawater, for example,

00:19:28.920 --> 00:19:30.200
is much faster than air.

00:19:30.200 --> 00:19:33.196
It's about 1,500
meters per second.

00:19:33.196 --> 00:19:35.030
And these techniques
are commonly

00:19:35.030 --> 00:19:38.930
used in sonars and depth
finders on ships and submarines.

00:19:38.930 --> 00:19:40.700
One of the important
distance measurements

00:19:40.700 --> 00:19:44.900
they make on this vessel is the
amount of water under the keel.

00:19:44.900 --> 00:19:47.690
Now, they actually have
several sensors on board

00:19:47.690 --> 00:19:51.590
the vessel that use
sonars or depth finders

00:19:51.590 --> 00:19:53.420
to figure out the distance.

00:19:53.420 --> 00:19:55.970
They all work on basically
the same principle.

00:19:55.970 --> 00:19:59.820
Over here, there's a depth
finder that says 22 feet.

00:19:59.820 --> 00:20:02.030
Now, the way they work
is an acoustic wave

00:20:02.030 --> 00:20:07.070
is sent from the bottom of the
ship down through the water.

00:20:07.070 --> 00:20:09.730
It bounces off of the
bottom of the harbor

00:20:09.730 --> 00:20:12.890
and is received by a transducer
in the bottom of the ship.

00:20:12.890 --> 00:20:15.860
By knowing the speed of sound
in the water and the round trip

00:20:15.860 --> 00:20:19.280
time, we can figure out
the depth under the keel.

00:20:19.280 --> 00:20:21.830
This radar unit uses
time-of-flight techniques

00:20:21.830 --> 00:20:23.820
with radio waves.

00:20:23.820 --> 00:20:26.075
This unit has about a
10-centimeter wavelength.

00:20:26.075 --> 00:20:30.440
And for wavelengths between
1 and 30 centimeters,

00:20:30.440 --> 00:20:32.420
we normally call
these microwaves.

00:20:32.420 --> 00:20:34.310
Now, basically,
the way this works

00:20:34.310 --> 00:20:37.310
is there's an
antenna up in a tower

00:20:37.310 --> 00:20:41.540
above this building, which
transmits radio waves out.

00:20:41.540 --> 00:20:44.120
And the radio waves are
reflected by airplanes

00:20:44.120 --> 00:20:47.000
flying around the Boston area.

00:20:47.000 --> 00:20:49.700
Because the radio waves
aren't attenuated in air

00:20:49.700 --> 00:20:52.670
as strongly as acoustic
waves, this technique

00:20:52.670 --> 00:20:54.920
is good for much
larger distances.

00:20:54.920 --> 00:20:57.630
Also the radio waves
travel much faster.

00:20:57.630 --> 00:21:00.110
So the radar, here, is
currently set for range

00:21:00.110 --> 00:21:02.560
of about 60 nautical miles.

00:21:02.560 --> 00:21:06.110
And it's common to use radars
at even longer distances.

00:21:06.110 --> 00:21:08.930
In fact, we measure the distance
between the Earth and planets

00:21:08.930 --> 00:21:10.730
using radar techniques.

00:21:10.730 --> 00:21:12.500
Now, so far, we've
only been talking

00:21:12.500 --> 00:21:13.850
about measuring distance.

00:21:13.850 --> 00:21:16.100
But you can see
in the radar plot

00:21:16.100 --> 00:21:20.120
that the radar also measures
the direction to the target.

00:21:20.120 --> 00:21:22.450
We do this by rotating
the radar antenna.

00:21:22.450 --> 00:21:25.970
And let's go up to the tower
and take a look at the antenna.

00:21:25.970 --> 00:21:28.570
I'm up here on the antenna
platform of this airport

00:21:28.570 --> 00:21:30.030
surveillance radar.

00:21:30.030 --> 00:21:32.150
And the antenna
above me is spinning

00:21:32.150 --> 00:21:36.860
at 4.8 seconds per revolution.

00:21:36.860 --> 00:21:40.280
Now, if I shut off the
antenna, it'll stop.

00:21:40.280 --> 00:21:44.450
We can take a look at how
the antenna actually works.

00:21:44.450 --> 00:21:46.730
Now, if you look
closely, you can

00:21:46.730 --> 00:21:49.990
see that there are
two transmitting

00:21:49.990 --> 00:21:57.260
and receive horns at the focus
of the parabolic antenna.

00:21:57.260 --> 00:22:00.740
Microwaves are emitted
from the transmitting horn.

00:22:00.740 --> 00:22:04.400
They go out and are
reflected off the parabola

00:22:04.400 --> 00:22:07.340
and focused into a
relatively narrow beam,

00:22:07.340 --> 00:22:10.770
at a beam width of
about 1.4 degrees.

00:22:10.770 --> 00:22:14.790
Now, the antenna also
works the other way, too.

00:22:14.790 --> 00:22:17.210
So microwaves reflected
off of targets

00:22:17.210 --> 00:22:20.780
will come back and be focused
into the receive horn.

00:22:20.780 --> 00:22:25.070
Now, you'll notice that the
antenna is wider horizontally

00:22:25.070 --> 00:22:26.656
than it is vertically.

00:22:26.656 --> 00:22:28.280
That's because, for
this type of radar,

00:22:28.280 --> 00:22:31.100
we actually want a
narrow beam horizontally.

00:22:31.100 --> 00:22:34.580
But we actually want a defocused
or broad beam vertically,

00:22:34.580 --> 00:22:37.090
so that we get what
we call a fan beam.

00:22:37.090 --> 00:22:41.780
And that allows us to scan
all altitudes at one rotation.

00:22:41.780 --> 00:22:45.890
Some types of radars or
some satellite receivers

00:22:45.890 --> 00:22:48.640
actually have a circular
parabolic antenna.

00:22:48.640 --> 00:22:51.230
And that gives a very tight
beam in both horizontally

00:22:51.230 --> 00:22:53.630
and vertically-- what
we call a pencil beam.

00:22:53.630 --> 00:22:57.530
Let's now take a look at a
very basic radar display.

00:22:57.530 --> 00:23:00.350
What I have here is what
we call Plan Position

00:23:00.350 --> 00:23:03.710
Indication display, or PPI.

00:23:03.710 --> 00:23:06.950
In the PPI, the
antenna location is

00:23:06.950 --> 00:23:08.820
at the center of the display.

00:23:08.820 --> 00:23:12.710
And you can see the
rotation of the antenna

00:23:12.710 --> 00:23:17.450
as it rotates in what we
call the azimuthal direction.

00:23:17.450 --> 00:23:20.840
Each of the aircraft targets
that the radar sees shows

00:23:20.840 --> 00:23:23.600
up as a what we call blip.

00:23:23.600 --> 00:23:26.990
And if you look
carefully at the blip,

00:23:26.990 --> 00:23:31.250
you can see some features that
indicate the uncertainties

00:23:31.250 --> 00:23:33.480
in the measurement.

00:23:33.480 --> 00:23:37.280
If you'll notice,
the targets show up

00:23:37.280 --> 00:23:40.970
wide in the azimuthal direction
but very narrow in the range

00:23:40.970 --> 00:23:42.760
direction.

00:23:42.760 --> 00:23:45.920
And what that tells you is
that the range measurement

00:23:45.920 --> 00:23:49.160
of the radar is much more
precise than the angle

00:23:49.160 --> 00:23:52.260
measurement or
azimuthal measurement.

00:23:52.260 --> 00:23:55.190
So the targets show up
as these sort of spread

00:23:55.190 --> 00:23:58.820
out targets in the
azimuthal direction.

00:23:58.820 --> 00:24:03.500
Now, this simple radar screen
just shows the primary returns

00:24:03.500 --> 00:24:05.330
from the targets.

00:24:05.330 --> 00:24:09.440
And if we look over here,
there's a more modern--

00:24:09.440 --> 00:24:12.380
in fact, this is a
research display--

00:24:12.380 --> 00:24:14.520
showing some of the
other information

00:24:14.520 --> 00:24:15.950
the air traffic controllers get.

00:24:15.950 --> 00:24:20.210
Now, let me orient you
to the display, here.

00:24:20.210 --> 00:24:22.610
Again, the radar
location, here, is

00:24:22.610 --> 00:24:24.750
at the center of the display.

00:24:24.750 --> 00:24:25.940
And you can see range rings.

00:24:25.940 --> 00:24:29.600
Here's one 10 nautical
miles out from the radar.

00:24:29.600 --> 00:24:34.200
Hanscom Airport is located
here, right next to the radar.

00:24:34.200 --> 00:24:36.860
And you can see the
runways at Hanscom.

00:24:36.860 --> 00:24:38.840
If you look down
to the right, you

00:24:38.840 --> 00:24:41.720
can see Boston Logan Airport,
again, the runways showing

00:24:41.720 --> 00:24:43.160
as white lines.

00:24:43.160 --> 00:24:45.020
And there are some
light gray lines,

00:24:45.020 --> 00:24:46.580
which show you
the coast and some

00:24:46.580 --> 00:24:49.490
of the main roads around Boston.

00:24:49.490 --> 00:24:53.030
Now, if we look at one
of the aircraft targets,

00:24:53.030 --> 00:24:55.550
you can see that there's
much more information here

00:24:55.550 --> 00:24:59.010
than just the blip we
saw on the other radar.

00:24:59.010 --> 00:25:03.490
First off, the best estimate
of the location of the target

00:25:03.490 --> 00:25:05.330
is shown.

00:25:05.330 --> 00:25:07.460
And there's some
secondary information

00:25:07.460 --> 00:25:09.270
you can see, over here.

00:25:09.270 --> 00:25:12.620
Now, most of the aircraft
flying around Boston

00:25:12.620 --> 00:25:16.850
actually have equipment on board
to enhance their radar returns.

00:25:16.850 --> 00:25:19.340
And this equipment's
called a transponder.

00:25:19.340 --> 00:25:22.790
And what it does is, when
it receives the radar

00:25:22.790 --> 00:25:25.270
pulse on board the
aircraft, there

00:25:25.270 --> 00:25:27.470
is a second transmitter
on the aircraft that

00:25:27.470 --> 00:25:29.780
sends a return
pulse, which is much

00:25:29.780 --> 00:25:33.080
stronger than the
basic reflection

00:25:33.080 --> 00:25:37.070
that you would have from what
we call a primary target.

00:25:37.070 --> 00:25:39.350
Now, along with that
pulse, the aircraft

00:25:39.350 --> 00:25:41.150
sends down some information.

00:25:41.150 --> 00:25:43.130
You can see this target here.

00:25:43.130 --> 00:25:45.440
The top line is the
identification number

00:25:45.440 --> 00:25:46.580
of the target.

00:25:46.580 --> 00:25:50.650
So it's sending
down a code of 5153.

00:25:50.650 --> 00:25:53.750
And below that
identification number

00:25:53.750 --> 00:25:57.360
are three digits, which indicate
the altitude of the target.

00:25:57.360 --> 00:26:02.150
So this aircraft is at
2,000 feet above sea level.

00:26:02.150 --> 00:26:06.500
This radar also
infers the velocity

00:26:06.500 --> 00:26:10.250
of the aircraft
from radar returns.

00:26:10.250 --> 00:26:13.560
So this little trail,
here, is essentially

00:26:13.560 --> 00:26:18.300
the last 10 hits that the
radar has had on the target.

00:26:18.300 --> 00:26:20.330
So again, that's
the last 48 seconds

00:26:20.330 --> 00:26:22.024
of data from the target.

00:26:22.024 --> 00:26:23.690
And by the way, I
should have mentioned,

00:26:23.690 --> 00:26:27.060
I've frozen the display
here so we could look at it.

00:26:27.060 --> 00:26:31.010
So based on those last
10 hits, the radar

00:26:31.010 --> 00:26:32.870
has calculated
that the target is

00:26:32.870 --> 00:26:35.960
moving at about 110
knots of ground speed.

00:26:35.960 --> 00:26:37.820
And it's moving to the west.

00:26:37.820 --> 00:26:42.350
So the air traffic controller
can figure out not only

00:26:42.350 --> 00:26:45.050
the location of the targets,
but where they're moving

00:26:45.050 --> 00:26:48.580
and if there's any
problems with collisions.

00:26:48.580 --> 00:26:52.740
Now, radars are used
for other applications.

00:26:52.740 --> 00:26:56.250
For example, we can use
radars to reflect off

00:26:56.250 --> 00:26:58.530
of rain and precipitation.

00:26:58.530 --> 00:27:01.200
And if you look
over here, you can

00:27:01.200 --> 00:27:04.680
see that I have a
computer output of a radar

00:27:04.680 --> 00:27:07.110
down in Florida, which
happens, this morning,

00:27:07.110 --> 00:27:10.590
to be looking at a hurricane
which is just hitting

00:27:10.590 --> 00:27:14.290
the panhandle of Florida.

00:27:14.290 --> 00:27:17.770
We can also use radars on
other vehicles, such as ships.

00:27:17.770 --> 00:27:21.400
And let's now go down
to the NOAA research

00:27:21.400 --> 00:27:23.590
vessel, Albatross, to look
at the radars on board

00:27:23.590 --> 00:27:25.040
that vessel.

00:27:25.040 --> 00:27:29.530
This is the radar display on
the bridge of the NOAA research

00:27:29.530 --> 00:27:32.590
vessel, Albatross IV.

00:27:32.590 --> 00:27:36.220
This is a display
of a primary radar,

00:27:36.220 --> 00:27:39.400
with the antennas mounted
on top of the bridge, here.

00:27:39.400 --> 00:27:42.310
As the radar rotates,
it sends out signals,

00:27:42.310 --> 00:27:46.170
which are bounced off of either
ship targets or land targets.

00:27:46.170 --> 00:27:48.220
And it can give us a picture.

00:27:48.220 --> 00:27:52.030
Now, the radar is actually used
for two purposes on the ship.

00:27:52.030 --> 00:27:53.635
One is to navigate,
and the other

00:27:53.635 --> 00:27:58.460
is to avoid colliding
with other ships or boats.

00:27:58.460 --> 00:28:04.540
We're now moored in Woods Hole,
Massachusetts, heading south.

00:28:04.540 --> 00:28:07.930
And I've set the display up here
at about a six nautical mile

00:28:07.930 --> 00:28:08.920
range.

00:28:08.920 --> 00:28:11.530
And you can see the coast
of Martha's Vineyard

00:28:11.530 --> 00:28:15.070
across Vineyard Sound, here.

00:28:15.070 --> 00:28:17.600
You can also see, if you
look closely-- in fact,

00:28:17.600 --> 00:28:21.100
let me increase the range,
adjust the display a little

00:28:21.100 --> 00:28:21.880
bit--

00:28:21.880 --> 00:28:29.050
you can see the black area is
the channel through Woods Hole.

00:28:29.050 --> 00:28:32.650
Now, as I said before, we also
use the radar to avoid targets.

00:28:32.650 --> 00:28:36.340
And the dots that
you see in the black

00:28:36.340 --> 00:28:39.460
are actually individual
ships or boats.

00:28:39.460 --> 00:28:41.500
If I increase the range
a little bit more,

00:28:41.500 --> 00:28:44.620
you can see a couple
things in the display.

00:28:44.620 --> 00:28:47.110
First, these straight
lines in front of us

00:28:47.110 --> 00:28:51.020
are actually the jetties
out in front of the ship.

00:28:51.020 --> 00:28:52.270
And you can see a few targets.

00:28:52.270 --> 00:28:54.040
I have one on the right, here.

00:28:54.040 --> 00:28:55.650
Let me mark it.

00:28:55.650 --> 00:28:56.650
There's a little cursor.

00:28:56.650 --> 00:29:01.270
And if I mark this
target, you can

00:29:01.270 --> 00:29:06.490
see that there's a boat 0.17
nautical miles to my right.

00:29:06.490 --> 00:29:10.270
It's on the 078 degree bearing.

00:29:10.270 --> 00:29:14.350
If we look out there, we can
see that the boat that I marked

00:29:14.350 --> 00:29:17.230
was actually the
12-meter yacht, Shamrock,

00:29:17.230 --> 00:29:21.280
which is moored in the harbor
next to us, here, at the dock.

00:29:21.280 --> 00:29:24.100
We've talked about techniques
for measuring distance using

00:29:24.100 --> 00:29:27.560
acoustic waves and radio waves.

00:29:27.560 --> 00:29:29.860
But you can also use
time-of-flight techniques

00:29:29.860 --> 00:29:34.384
with light or
infrared radiation.

00:29:34.384 --> 00:29:35.800
And in fact, much
of the equipment

00:29:35.800 --> 00:29:39.540
that's used for land surveying
uses time-of-flight techniques

00:29:39.540 --> 00:29:41.740
of infrared radiation.

00:29:41.740 --> 00:29:43.690
Now, I made
arrangements with one

00:29:43.690 --> 00:29:46.660
of the surveyors who
works with MIT to show you

00:29:46.660 --> 00:29:49.870
this equipment by measuring
one of the buildings at MIT.

00:29:49.870 --> 00:29:53.140
Distance measurement is an
important part of surveying.

00:29:53.140 --> 00:29:55.690
And Ken Strom, who's
a professional land

00:29:55.690 --> 00:29:58.090
surveyor for Cullen
Engineering, has

00:29:58.090 --> 00:30:02.295
agreed to help us do a
survey problem here at MIT.

00:30:02.295 --> 00:30:04.420
What I'd like to do-- what
I thought would be fun--

00:30:04.420 --> 00:30:07.600
is to try to measure the exact
height of the Great Dome,

00:30:07.600 --> 00:30:08.475
here, behind me.

00:30:08.475 --> 00:30:10.600
So, Ken, how do we have
to-- what do we have to do,

00:30:10.600 --> 00:30:11.500
to do this?

00:30:11.500 --> 00:30:14.560
STROM: Well, what we did was we
established a point over here,

00:30:14.560 --> 00:30:16.760
which has a known position.

00:30:16.760 --> 00:30:19.870
And we also established
an elevation on that point

00:30:19.870 --> 00:30:21.850
by turning on it, using
differential leveling

00:30:21.850 --> 00:30:24.190
techniques from a
deep benchmark, which

00:30:24.190 --> 00:30:27.370
was established in 1927,
located near the corner

00:30:27.370 --> 00:30:30.502
of this building
right behind us, here.

00:30:30.502 --> 00:30:31.270
HANSMAN: Okay.

00:30:31.270 --> 00:30:33.580
So we know the
height of this point.

00:30:33.580 --> 00:30:36.460
And we can then measure the
difference between this point

00:30:36.460 --> 00:30:37.540
and the top of the Dome.

00:30:37.540 --> 00:30:38.040
STROM: Yes.

00:30:38.040 --> 00:30:40.390
Utilizing our total
station instrument,

00:30:40.390 --> 00:30:44.320
we can establish a difference in
elevation between the sidewalk,

00:30:44.320 --> 00:30:45.820
here, and the top of the dome.

00:30:45.820 --> 00:30:48.460
HANSMAN: Surveying
requires a measurement

00:30:48.460 --> 00:30:51.550
of both the distance
and direction

00:30:51.550 --> 00:30:54.910
from the known point,
or reference mark,

00:30:54.910 --> 00:30:56.860
to the point you're
trying to measure.

00:30:56.860 --> 00:30:59.290
Traditionally, we would use
something like this steel tape

00:30:59.290 --> 00:31:01.930
to measure the distance
and an instrument

00:31:01.930 --> 00:31:07.240
called a theodolite to measure
the direction of the distance.

00:31:07.240 --> 00:31:09.370
Nowadays, it's very common
to use something called

00:31:09.370 --> 00:31:11.650
a total station
instrument, which

00:31:11.650 --> 00:31:13.330
combines an
electronic measurement

00:31:13.330 --> 00:31:15.970
of the distance with an
electronic measurement

00:31:15.970 --> 00:31:18.880
of the angles.

00:31:18.880 --> 00:31:22.330
Now, the way this
particular unit works is it

00:31:22.330 --> 00:31:27.280
has an infrared signal, which
is emitted from these optics.

00:31:27.280 --> 00:31:31.450
The wavelength of the infrared
signal is about 0.9 microns.

00:31:31.450 --> 00:31:35.170
And that wavelength is chosen
for eye safety reasons.

00:31:35.170 --> 00:31:40.100
What happens is the infrared
signal comes out from the unit

00:31:40.100 --> 00:31:42.730
and is reflected
back by a reflector

00:31:42.730 --> 00:31:45.760
that we set at the
measurement point.

00:31:45.760 --> 00:31:50.160
This particular reflector,
here, is a prism.

00:31:50.160 --> 00:31:54.520
And so we measure the
distance from the instrument

00:31:54.520 --> 00:31:56.740
to the reflector by
measuring the time

00:31:56.740 --> 00:31:59.980
it takes the infrared signal
to go out to the reflector

00:31:59.980 --> 00:32:01.280
and go back.

00:32:01.280 --> 00:32:05.950
And that round trip time is,
essentially, the distance

00:32:05.950 --> 00:32:09.160
divided by the speed of light.

00:32:09.160 --> 00:32:13.000
Now, the speed of light
in air is the speed

00:32:13.000 --> 00:32:16.340
of light in vacuum, about 3
times 10 to the eighth meters

00:32:16.340 --> 00:32:22.417
per second, divided by the index
of refraction of in the air.

00:32:22.417 --> 00:32:24.500
And so we have to correct
for both the temperature

00:32:24.500 --> 00:32:27.150
and pressure, which influence
the index of refraction.

00:32:27.150 --> 00:32:30.710
Now, the reason
why we use a prism

00:32:30.710 --> 00:32:33.920
is that we want to make sure
that we have a good signal

00:32:33.920 --> 00:32:35.450
going back to the instrument.

00:32:35.450 --> 00:32:38.870
If I use something just like a
mirror, if it was misaligned,

00:32:38.870 --> 00:32:40.800
I might run into a problem.

00:32:40.800 --> 00:32:43.870
So this prism is what
we call a corner cube.

00:32:43.870 --> 00:32:46.670
If we send the light
into the corner,

00:32:46.670 --> 00:32:48.890
no matter which
direction it comes from,

00:32:48.890 --> 00:32:51.620
it'll go straight back
out at the instrument.

00:32:51.620 --> 00:32:53.100
It's a very common technique.

00:32:53.100 --> 00:32:56.900
In fact, there are some corner
cubes set up on the moon

00:32:56.900 --> 00:32:58.940
that we use to
measure the distance

00:32:58.940 --> 00:33:00.750
between the Earth and the moon.

00:33:00.750 --> 00:33:04.520
So, Ken, what we
need to do here is

00:33:04.520 --> 00:33:06.560
find the height
of the instrument

00:33:06.560 --> 00:33:10.400
before I go up in the
Dome to set up the prism.

00:33:10.400 --> 00:33:11.000
STROM: Yes.

00:33:11.000 --> 00:33:13.670
As we mentioned earlier,
we established an elevation

00:33:13.670 --> 00:33:16.400
of our reference
mark with relation

00:33:16.400 --> 00:33:18.200
to mean sea level datum.

00:33:18.200 --> 00:33:20.810
And that was done via
differential leveling

00:33:20.810 --> 00:33:22.130
techniques.

00:33:22.130 --> 00:33:25.910
And we've established an
elevation of 10.02 feet

00:33:25.910 --> 00:33:27.530
on the reference mark.

00:33:27.530 --> 00:33:30.170
And what we will do is
measure up with a ruler

00:33:30.170 --> 00:33:32.390
to an index mark
on the instrument.

00:33:32.390 --> 00:33:35.900
And that will-- we're
measuring 5.36 feet

00:33:35.900 --> 00:33:38.300
above the reference
mark, which establishes

00:33:38.300 --> 00:33:42.680
an elevation of the instrument
of 15.38 feet above mean sea

00:33:42.680 --> 00:33:43.780
level.

00:33:43.780 --> 00:33:45.020
HANSMAN: Okay.

00:33:45.020 --> 00:33:46.220
I've got the prism up here.

00:33:46.220 --> 00:33:48.810
I actually had to
pull it up on a line.

00:33:48.810 --> 00:33:51.350
And I set it up on
this tripod, which

00:33:51.350 --> 00:33:53.830
is right over the
middle of the Dome.

00:33:53.830 --> 00:33:57.880
And what I'll do is I'll
try and align it to Ken.

00:33:57.880 --> 00:34:01.170
And, Ken, do you have a good
line of sight to the prism?

00:34:07.390 --> 00:34:09.870
Ken, do you have a good
line of sight to the prism?

00:34:09.870 --> 00:34:12.380
STROM: John, I can see
you through my crosshairs.

00:34:12.380 --> 00:34:13.190
Over.

00:34:13.190 --> 00:34:13.480
HANSMAN: All right.

00:34:13.480 --> 00:34:13.929
Good.

00:34:13.929 --> 00:34:14.428
All right.

00:34:14.428 --> 00:34:15.639
I'll just secure it here.

00:34:15.639 --> 00:34:18.432
And I'll be down in a minute
to make the measurement.

00:34:18.432 --> 00:34:19.294
STROM: Okay.

00:34:19.294 --> 00:34:20.650
HANSMAN: Okay.

00:34:20.650 --> 00:34:22.270
I think we're stable.

00:34:22.270 --> 00:34:24.912
It's pretty windy up here,
so we've tied down the prism.

00:34:24.912 --> 00:34:25.870
And I'll head down now.

00:34:25.870 --> 00:34:28.630
And we'll see what the
height of this place is.

00:34:31.610 --> 00:34:32.110
Okay.

00:34:32.110 --> 00:34:35.699
Ken, now that I have the prism
set up on top of the Dome,

00:34:35.699 --> 00:34:38.400
we need to line up the
instrument on the prism?

00:34:38.400 --> 00:34:39.280
STROM: Yes.

00:34:44.469 --> 00:34:44.969
Yes.

00:34:44.969 --> 00:34:46.510
The prism, now, is
in the crosshairs.

00:34:46.510 --> 00:34:47.190
HANSMAN: Okay.

00:34:47.190 --> 00:34:50.969
And if we press this
angle button, what we see

00:34:50.969 --> 00:34:56.860
is that the angle of the line
of sight from the vertical,

00:34:56.860 --> 00:34:59.150
which we call the
zenith angle, is what?

00:34:59.150 --> 00:35:03.270
STROM: 77 degrees, 44
minutes, and 5 seconds.

00:35:03.270 --> 00:35:04.110
HANSMAN: Okay.

00:35:04.110 --> 00:35:06.932
And if we make the
range measurement.

00:35:10.096 --> 00:35:10.730
STROM: Yes.

00:35:10.730 --> 00:35:14.810
The slope measurement
is 693.215 feet.

00:35:14.810 --> 00:35:17.570
HANSMAN: So that's the
distance between the instrument

00:35:17.570 --> 00:35:18.710
and the prism.

00:35:18.710 --> 00:35:19.250
STROM: Yes.

00:35:19.250 --> 00:35:22.940
HANSMAN: And the
actual height is

00:35:22.940 --> 00:35:27.820
that distance times the
cosine of the zenith angle--

00:35:27.820 --> 00:35:28.445
STROM: Correct.

00:35:28.445 --> 00:35:32.630
HANSMAN: --which
now is 147.275 feet.

00:35:32.630 --> 00:35:34.450
Before I take down
the prism, I figured

00:35:34.450 --> 00:35:36.380
we might as well take a look
around while we're up here,

00:35:36.380 --> 00:35:37.580
on top of the Great Dome.

00:35:37.580 --> 00:35:40.670
You can see the Green
Building at MIT.

00:35:40.670 --> 00:35:42.470
And across the
Charles River, you

00:35:42.470 --> 00:35:44.940
can see the skyline of Boston.

00:35:44.940 --> 00:35:49.280
There's the Hancock Tower
and the Prudential Tower.

00:35:49.280 --> 00:35:52.250
And down there, you can
actually see the Mass Ave

00:35:52.250 --> 00:35:54.320
Bridge, or the Harvard Bridge.

00:35:54.320 --> 00:35:55.940
Well, when we had
Ken here, I couldn't

00:35:55.940 --> 00:35:59.030
resist asking him to come
out to the Harvard Bridge

00:35:59.030 --> 00:36:02.220
to see if we could see how
long the bridge is in feet,

00:36:02.220 --> 00:36:04.880
to compare that with Smoots.

00:36:04.880 --> 00:36:06.620
Because the bridge is
curved, we actually

00:36:06.620 --> 00:36:09.140
had to set up the station
in the middle of the bridge.

00:36:09.140 --> 00:36:10.974
And we put prisms on both ends.

00:36:10.974 --> 00:36:12.890
So, Ken, why don't you
go ahead and see if you

00:36:12.890 --> 00:36:14.450
can measure the first prism.

00:36:18.716 --> 00:36:22.147
STROM: 1,014.53 feet.

00:36:22.147 --> 00:36:22.730
HANSMAN: Okay.

00:36:22.730 --> 00:36:24.890
So that's the distance
from about the middle

00:36:24.890 --> 00:36:26.120
of the bridge to the other--

00:36:26.120 --> 00:36:27.369
to the north end.

00:36:27.369 --> 00:36:28.910
And what we'll do
is we'll switch it.

00:36:28.910 --> 00:36:30.270
And we'll shoot the other side.

00:36:35.590 --> 00:36:36.090
Okay.

00:36:39.817 --> 00:36:41.900
Then we'll go ahead and
make the measurement here.

00:36:46.270 --> 00:36:46.770
Okay.

00:36:46.770 --> 00:36:48.180
I got it lined up.

00:36:48.180 --> 00:36:50.630
Let me measure.

00:36:50.630 --> 00:36:55.092
Okay, Ken, it's 1,008.27 feet.

00:36:55.092 --> 00:36:57.350
STROM: 1,008.27.

00:36:57.350 --> 00:36:58.100
HANSMAN: Okay.

00:36:58.100 --> 00:36:59.870
So the total length
of the bridge is--

00:36:59.870 --> 00:37:04.255
STROM: We had 1,014.53
heading towards Cambridge.

00:37:04.255 --> 00:37:08.827
So the total would
be 2,022.8 feet.

00:37:08.827 --> 00:37:09.410
HANSMAN: Okay.

00:37:09.410 --> 00:37:12.330
So and that's equal to 3--

00:37:12.330 --> 00:37:14.800
STROM: 364.4 Smoots.

00:37:14.800 --> 00:37:15.751
HANSMAN: And an ear.

00:37:15.751 --> 00:37:16.250
STROM: Yes.

00:37:16.250 --> 00:37:17.410
One ear.

00:37:17.410 --> 00:37:20.600
HANSMAN: One very important
type of distance measurement

00:37:20.600 --> 00:37:24.260
is navigation or locating
your position in space.

00:37:24.260 --> 00:37:25.850
Now, in order to
do this, you need

00:37:25.850 --> 00:37:28.040
some sort of coordinate system.

00:37:28.040 --> 00:37:31.100
On the surface of the
Earth, we use, essentially,

00:37:31.100 --> 00:37:33.860
a spherical coordinate
system because the earth

00:37:33.860 --> 00:37:36.960
is approximately a sphere.

00:37:36.960 --> 00:37:40.820
Now the system we use is the
latitude-longitude system.

00:37:40.820 --> 00:37:45.580
0 degrees latitude is,
essentially, the equator.

00:37:45.580 --> 00:37:48.170
And it rotates in
angular measurement,

00:37:48.170 --> 00:37:50.870
from the center of the Earth
up to 90 degrees at the North

00:37:50.870 --> 00:37:55.850
Pole, and 90 degrees
south at the South Pole.

00:37:55.850 --> 00:38:03.680
Now, longitude is defined in
terms of east-west position.

00:38:03.680 --> 00:38:06.570
And 0 degrees longitude
is in Greenwich, England,

00:38:06.570 --> 00:38:08.540
very close to London.

00:38:08.540 --> 00:38:11.840
And again, longitude
goes east and west,

00:38:11.840 --> 00:38:15.750
from that 0 position to 180
degrees at the International

00:38:15.750 --> 00:38:19.190
Date Line in the
middle of the Pacific.

00:38:19.190 --> 00:38:22.990
Now, our location,
here in Boston, is--

00:38:22.990 --> 00:38:24.760
let's find Greenwich again--

00:38:24.760 --> 00:38:28.890
is about 70 degrees west
of Greenwich, England,

00:38:28.890 --> 00:38:33.050
and again, about 45 degrees
north from the equator.

00:38:33.050 --> 00:38:36.910
So our latitude is
45 degrees north,

00:38:36.910 --> 00:38:40.620
and our longitude is
about 70 degrees west.

00:38:40.620 --> 00:38:42.450
Now, there are
many different ways

00:38:42.450 --> 00:38:48.270
we can measure position, ranging
from celestial navigation

00:38:48.270 --> 00:38:51.780
techniques, where we locate
our position on the Earth

00:38:51.780 --> 00:38:55.680
with regard to stars, such as
the North Star or other stars

00:38:55.680 --> 00:38:57.600
in the sky at night.

00:38:57.600 --> 00:39:00.360
We can also use radio
navigation techniques,

00:39:00.360 --> 00:39:05.370
where we measure our position
with regard to radio beacons

00:39:05.370 --> 00:39:06.810
on the Earth.

00:39:06.810 --> 00:39:09.120
But perhaps the most
important new technique

00:39:09.120 --> 00:39:12.330
is something that's called
a global positioning system.

00:39:12.330 --> 00:39:14.620
This is a small Global
Positioning System,

00:39:14.620 --> 00:39:16.320
or GPS, receiver.

00:39:16.320 --> 00:39:19.350
Basically, GPS is another
time-of-flight technique

00:39:19.350 --> 00:39:21.930
using radio waves.

00:39:21.930 --> 00:39:25.080
But the transmitters are
mounted on a constellation

00:39:25.080 --> 00:39:28.500
of satellites, which
orbits the Earth.

00:39:28.500 --> 00:39:30.630
Right now, there are
about 24 satellites

00:39:30.630 --> 00:39:31.740
in the constellation.

00:39:31.740 --> 00:39:35.610
And they're designed such
that, at any one time,

00:39:35.610 --> 00:39:37.680
you should be able to see
somewhere between four

00:39:37.680 --> 00:39:39.030
and eight satellites.

00:39:39.030 --> 00:39:41.490
A computer in the receiver
knows where all the satellites

00:39:41.490 --> 00:39:44.020
should be at any one time.

00:39:44.020 --> 00:39:47.100
So by measuring the distance
to several satellites,

00:39:47.100 --> 00:39:49.350
you can figure
out your location.

00:39:49.350 --> 00:39:52.080
Now, you actually need
to measure the distance

00:39:52.080 --> 00:39:54.090
to at least four satellites.

00:39:54.090 --> 00:39:57.300
And that's because the
clock in the receiver

00:39:57.300 --> 00:40:00.690
is not as accurate as
the clocks on satellites.

00:40:00.690 --> 00:40:03.630
So by making four
measurements, we

00:40:03.630 --> 00:40:06.780
can resolve the three
components of position--

00:40:06.780 --> 00:40:08.970
say, north, east, and down--

00:40:08.970 --> 00:40:14.310
and also correct any uncertainty
in the receiver clock.

00:40:14.310 --> 00:40:18.470
Now, the accuracy of the
GPS depends on the geometry

00:40:18.470 --> 00:40:20.330
of the satellites.

00:40:20.330 --> 00:40:21.890
In order to get a
good measurement,

00:40:21.890 --> 00:40:24.380
you'd really like the
satellites to be spread out.

00:40:24.380 --> 00:40:27.380
If all the satellites
were in the same position,

00:40:27.380 --> 00:40:29.750
you'd essentially just be
making the same measurement

00:40:29.750 --> 00:40:31.250
four times.

00:40:31.250 --> 00:40:34.730
So you can see in
this figure here,

00:40:34.730 --> 00:40:39.030
if we just look
at two satellites,

00:40:39.030 --> 00:40:41.175
if I'm a certain range
from satellite A,

00:40:41.175 --> 00:40:45.750
I could be anywhere on this
line with some uncertainty.

00:40:45.750 --> 00:40:50.100
Now, if I measure from satellite
B, I could be on this line.

00:40:50.100 --> 00:40:53.340
And my position would
be in the region

00:40:53.340 --> 00:40:56.940
covered by the intersection
of the two lines.

00:40:56.940 --> 00:40:59.670
But I could be anywhere within
the intersection of the two

00:40:59.670 --> 00:41:01.830
uncertainty bands.

00:41:01.830 --> 00:41:04.060
Now, if the satellites
were close together,

00:41:04.060 --> 00:41:06.060
you can see the
region of uncertainty

00:41:06.060 --> 00:41:07.830
becomes much larger.

00:41:07.830 --> 00:41:09.810
And this effect is
called the Geometric

00:41:09.810 --> 00:41:12.215
Dilution of Precision, or GDOP.

00:41:12.215 --> 00:41:15.430
And it's normally
monitored by the receiver.

00:41:15.430 --> 00:41:18.120
Now, if the GDOP is
too large, or if you

00:41:18.120 --> 00:41:21.120
can't see enough satellites,
then your receiver

00:41:21.120 --> 00:41:23.850
should warn you that your
measurement of position

00:41:23.850 --> 00:41:25.704
is unreliable.

00:41:25.704 --> 00:41:27.120
Now, there are
other factors which

00:41:27.120 --> 00:41:31.410
can influence the precision
of a GPS measurement.

00:41:31.410 --> 00:41:35.970
And one of those is the
accuracy of the receiver.

00:41:35.970 --> 00:41:41.490
Now, this receiver uses what's
called a civil, or C/A, code.

00:41:41.490 --> 00:41:45.150
And most civil
receivers use that code.

00:41:45.150 --> 00:41:49.710
The military uses another
code, called the P code.

00:41:49.710 --> 00:41:52.230
And the civil C/A
code is intentionally

00:41:52.230 --> 00:41:56.410
degraded by the military
for political reasons.

00:41:56.410 --> 00:41:59.400
So that measurement
will only be accurate to

00:41:59.400 --> 00:42:01.340
within a few hundred feet.

00:42:01.340 --> 00:42:04.500
And we actually checked this
by taking this receiver up

00:42:04.500 --> 00:42:08.730
on top of the Great Dome
to measure the GPS position

00:42:08.730 --> 00:42:11.400
and compare it with
Ken's surveyed position.

00:42:11.400 --> 00:42:16.350
This is a civil GPS unit, so
it only uses the C/A code.

00:42:16.350 --> 00:42:17.580
You can see, here--

00:42:17.580 --> 00:42:18.420
here's the antenna.

00:42:18.420 --> 00:42:20.160
Here's the unit--
right now there

00:42:20.160 --> 00:42:24.440
are six satellites available
to us and potentially in view.

00:42:24.440 --> 00:42:27.870
And the GPS is locked up
on all six satellites.

00:42:27.870 --> 00:42:29.730
Okay.

00:42:29.730 --> 00:42:32.970
So what we'll do is we'll now
press the navigation page.

00:42:32.970 --> 00:42:36.540
And we can see that
the GPS only thinks

00:42:36.540 --> 00:42:38.402
we're 37 feet above sea level.

00:42:38.402 --> 00:42:40.860
You'll see that drift around
a little bit as the satellites

00:42:40.860 --> 00:42:41.880
move.

00:42:41.880 --> 00:42:45.370
However, the latitude and
longitude are also displayed.

00:42:45.370 --> 00:42:51.720
We're 42 degrees, 21 minutes, 58
seconds north, and 71 degrees,

00:42:51.720 --> 00:42:56.070
5 minutes, 53 seconds
west according to the GPS.

00:42:56.070 --> 00:42:59.850
We can also see how far we
are from a reference point.

00:42:59.850 --> 00:43:02.360
Right now, we have
Logan Airport in here.

00:43:02.360 --> 00:43:07.560
And Logan Airport is 3.58
nautical miles just about due

00:43:07.560 --> 00:43:09.850
east of us, 0.80 degrees.

00:43:09.850 --> 00:43:13.920
So in fact, if you
look at it, you'll

00:43:13.920 --> 00:43:16.530
be able to see
the tower of Logan

00:43:16.530 --> 00:43:19.380
Airport, off in the distance.

00:43:19.380 --> 00:43:22.980
When I compared the GPS-measured
coordinates of the Great

00:43:22.980 --> 00:43:26.340
Dome with the surveyed
coordinates that Ken gave me,

00:43:26.340 --> 00:43:29.550
I found that the GPS located
the Great Dome a few hundred

00:43:29.550 --> 00:43:33.780
feet north and west
of Ken's location.

00:43:33.780 --> 00:43:35.340
And most of this
difference was due

00:43:35.340 --> 00:43:38.160
to the selective
availability effect.

00:43:38.160 --> 00:43:39.930
Now, there are
ways we can correct

00:43:39.930 --> 00:43:43.060
for selective availability
and other errors.

00:43:43.060 --> 00:43:46.680
And one of these is something
called differential GPS.

00:43:46.680 --> 00:43:49.080
Now, with differential
GPS, what you do

00:43:49.080 --> 00:43:54.780
is you have another GPS receiver
at a known surveyed location.

00:43:54.780 --> 00:44:00.030
And what it does is essentially
calculates the current error

00:44:00.030 --> 00:44:03.060
due to selective availability.

00:44:03.060 --> 00:44:05.070
It then transmits
this correction up

00:44:05.070 --> 00:44:11.460
to the operational GPS receiver
so that you can locate position

00:44:11.460 --> 00:44:12.620
much more accurately.

00:44:12.620 --> 00:44:15.240
Basically, you're
just compensating out

00:44:15.240 --> 00:44:17.610
the selective
availability effect.

00:44:17.610 --> 00:44:21.360
With differential GPS, it's
possible to get your position

00:44:21.360 --> 00:44:23.670
to within a few feet
if you're fairly

00:44:23.670 --> 00:44:28.060
close to the known
surveyed receiver.

00:44:28.060 --> 00:44:29.640
Now, you can even do better.

00:44:29.640 --> 00:44:32.790
You can get accuracies as
high as a few centimeters

00:44:32.790 --> 00:44:39.720
or a few millimeters if you use
interferometric GPS techniques.

00:44:39.720 --> 00:44:43.410
Now, we've talked about a lot of
distance measuring techniques.

00:44:43.410 --> 00:44:45.480
And what I'd like
to do now is go out

00:44:45.480 --> 00:44:49.240
and show you a simple example
of a velocity measurement

00:44:49.240 --> 00:44:49.932
technique.

00:44:53.240 --> 00:44:55.220
Every time you drive
a car, you actually

00:44:55.220 --> 00:44:57.380
see a velocity measurement
on the speedometer

00:44:57.380 --> 00:44:58.670
in front of you.

00:44:58.670 --> 00:45:00.740
Now, my bike has an
odometer, which actually

00:45:00.740 --> 00:45:02.303
works on the same principle.

00:45:05.930 --> 00:45:08.030
Now, the way most
odometers work is

00:45:08.030 --> 00:45:11.730
by counting the number of
rotations of the wheel.

00:45:11.730 --> 00:45:16.580
In this case, on my bike here,
I have a magnetics pickup.

00:45:16.580 --> 00:45:20.400
There's a small magnet
attached to the spoke.

00:45:20.400 --> 00:45:22.700
And there's a
magnetic sensor, here.

00:45:22.700 --> 00:45:25.610
Every time the magnet
goes by the sensor,

00:45:25.610 --> 00:45:30.500
it creates a little
voltage pulse.

00:45:30.500 --> 00:45:33.800
And that pulse is sent up this
wire to the computer unit,

00:45:33.800 --> 00:45:35.060
here.

00:45:35.060 --> 00:45:38.840
Now, the computer unit
has a quartz clock in it.

00:45:38.840 --> 00:45:41.810
And the quartz
clock, essentially,

00:45:41.810 --> 00:45:44.310
counts the number of
pulses per second.

00:45:44.310 --> 00:45:46.310
If I know the
circumference of the wheel,

00:45:46.310 --> 00:45:48.450
I can figure out
what the velocity is.

00:45:48.450 --> 00:45:52.250
So for example, if
I spin the wheel,

00:45:52.250 --> 00:45:55.540
you can see that there is an
indication of pulses, here.

00:45:55.540 --> 00:45:58.250
And I get an
indication of velocity.

00:45:58.250 --> 00:46:00.590
I can also integrate
the number of pulses,

00:46:00.590 --> 00:46:02.910
and I can get a total
distance covered.

00:46:02.910 --> 00:46:06.200
In fact, on this ride,
I've gone about 12.1 miles.

00:46:06.200 --> 00:46:07.980
I can accumulate that over time.

00:46:07.980 --> 00:46:12.050
So for example, the total number
of miles I've ridden this bike

00:46:12.050 --> 00:46:14.270
is about 1,540.

00:46:14.270 --> 00:46:16.890
Now, we can measure velocity
using other techniques.

00:46:16.890 --> 00:46:20.330
For example, we can
measure velocity

00:46:20.330 --> 00:46:22.440
by differentiating
position measurements.

00:46:22.440 --> 00:46:25.370
So for example, if
we take the GPS unit

00:46:25.370 --> 00:46:29.450
and differentiate position, we
can get a velocity measurement.

00:46:29.450 --> 00:46:32.287
At the 25 or 30 miles an
hour I max out on my bike,

00:46:32.287 --> 00:46:34.370
there's not much risk I'm
going to get pulled over

00:46:34.370 --> 00:46:35.504
for speeding.

00:46:35.504 --> 00:46:37.670
However, it's interesting
to see how police actually

00:46:37.670 --> 00:46:39.056
measure velocity.

00:46:39.056 --> 00:46:40.430
Let's go take a
look at that now.

00:46:43.340 --> 00:46:46.520
We're out here on Memorial
Drive in front of MIT,

00:46:46.520 --> 00:46:49.670
where I've enlisted the help
of Sergeant Eric Anderson,

00:46:49.670 --> 00:46:51.590
from the Massachusetts
State Police,

00:46:51.590 --> 00:46:54.590
to show us some of the different
velocity-measurement techniques

00:46:54.590 --> 00:46:56.030
that are commonly used.

00:46:56.030 --> 00:46:59.000
We're going to look at both
a Doppler radar technique

00:46:59.000 --> 00:47:01.920
and a LIDAR technique.

00:47:01.920 --> 00:47:05.690
This is a standard
Doppler radar unit.

00:47:05.690 --> 00:47:08.860
And the way it works is by
using the Doppler effect.

00:47:08.860 --> 00:47:11.390
Now, what the
Doppler effect is, is

00:47:11.390 --> 00:47:17.300
a shift in frequency caused by
the relative motion of objects.

00:47:17.300 --> 00:47:19.700
And you're maybe familiar
with this if you've ever heard

00:47:19.700 --> 00:47:21.860
a train whistle come at you.

00:47:21.860 --> 00:47:25.310
You'll hear the tone
change as it comes at you

00:47:25.310 --> 00:47:26.570
and then goes away.

00:47:26.570 --> 00:47:29.440
You can almost hear it in
some of these cars going by.

00:47:29.440 --> 00:47:32.780
The tone of the cars
changes as it goes by.

00:47:32.780 --> 00:47:36.410
Now, this same effect occurs
with electromagnetic waves.

00:47:36.410 --> 00:47:40.220
And this unit uses a
microwave with a wavelength

00:47:40.220 --> 00:47:42.040
of about 1 centimeter.

00:47:42.040 --> 00:47:46.770
And that's a frequency
of about 24 gigahertz.

00:47:46.770 --> 00:47:49.130
Now, what happens
is the wave goes out

00:47:49.130 --> 00:47:51.610
from the gun to the
approaching vehicle

00:47:51.610 --> 00:47:54.830
and is reflected off
the approaching vehicle.

00:47:54.830 --> 00:47:57.110
The vehicle sees
a Doppler shift.

00:47:57.110 --> 00:47:58.820
And the amount of
the Doppler shift

00:47:58.820 --> 00:48:02.570
is proportional to the
velocity of the car divided

00:48:02.570 --> 00:48:03.870
by the speed of light.

00:48:03.870 --> 00:48:06.680
So what the unit does is
actually very carefully

00:48:06.680 --> 00:48:10.460
compares the outgoing frequency
to the return frequency

00:48:10.460 --> 00:48:12.830
and then measures speed.

00:48:12.830 --> 00:48:17.180
Now, there is a little bit of a
problem with this unit, in that

00:48:17.180 --> 00:48:19.570
it's got a fairly wide beam.

00:48:19.570 --> 00:48:22.800
The beam width is
about 12 degrees.

00:48:22.800 --> 00:48:25.790
So it sort of sprays
out over the whole road.

00:48:25.790 --> 00:48:28.340
That's okay, because
what it will do

00:48:28.340 --> 00:48:31.620
is it will lock onto the
largest signal that's returning.

00:48:31.620 --> 00:48:34.700
That'll either be the closest
vehicle or, in some cases,

00:48:34.700 --> 00:48:35.584
the biggest vehicle.

00:48:35.584 --> 00:48:38.000
If you have a bus or something
like that, what will happen

00:48:38.000 --> 00:48:41.750
is you'll get a
large radar return.

00:48:41.750 --> 00:48:43.610
But it's a very good unit.

00:48:43.610 --> 00:48:45.170
There are some
other units that are

00:48:45.170 --> 00:48:47.090
used that have actually
tighter beam widths.

00:48:47.090 --> 00:48:49.160
And that's what the
advantage of the laser unit.

00:48:49.160 --> 00:48:50.850
Let's take a look at that now.

00:48:50.850 --> 00:48:56.240
ANDERSON: Trooper White is now
utilizing the laser unit, which

00:48:56.240 --> 00:49:00.110
is a relatively recent
speed-measuring technology

00:49:00.110 --> 00:49:02.960
for the law
enforcement community.

00:49:02.960 --> 00:49:05.200
We really like it because
it's officer-interactive.

00:49:05.200 --> 00:49:08.241
And the width of the
beam is more specific.

00:49:08.241 --> 00:49:10.490
So we're actually targeting
in on the specific vehicle

00:49:10.490 --> 00:49:11.467
without question.

00:49:11.467 --> 00:49:12.050
HANSMAN: Yeah.

00:49:12.050 --> 00:49:13.950
The laser unit actually is--

00:49:13.950 --> 00:49:17.240
even though it's more modern--
it's simpler in principle.

00:49:17.240 --> 00:49:21.300
What it does is it uses a
time-of-flight technique

00:49:21.300 --> 00:49:23.960
and makes a range
measurement to the vehicle.

00:49:23.960 --> 00:49:27.800
What you do is it sends out
an infrared pulse, which

00:49:27.800 --> 00:49:30.190
goes out and bounces off
the vehicle and comes back.

00:49:30.190 --> 00:49:33.020
And by knowing the speed
of light and the time

00:49:33.020 --> 00:49:35.000
it took the pulse to
go out and come back,

00:49:35.000 --> 00:49:37.370
you can measure the
distance to the vehicle.

00:49:37.370 --> 00:49:39.940
Then what you do is you measure
the distance several times

00:49:39.940 --> 00:49:42.787
and get an estimate of the
velocity of the vehicle.

00:49:42.787 --> 00:49:44.370
You've got to be a
little bit careful.

00:49:44.370 --> 00:49:45.911
You can't just do
it with two pulses,

00:49:45.911 --> 00:49:49.190
because you might first get a
hit on the front of the vehicle

00:49:49.190 --> 00:49:52.050
and then get one on the
mirror or something like that.

00:49:52.050 --> 00:49:54.450
So that would cause an
error in the measurement.

00:49:54.450 --> 00:49:57.830
And what these units do is
make a lot of measurements--

00:49:57.830 --> 00:49:59.600
about 360 a second--

00:49:59.600 --> 00:50:03.950
to get an accurate
measurement of the velocity.

00:50:03.950 --> 00:50:08.090
This gun operates at a
wavelength of 0.9 microns.

00:50:08.090 --> 00:50:11.600
And the reason it does that
is for eye safety reasons.

00:50:11.600 --> 00:50:14.450
If you were to go to
shorter, visible wavelengths,

00:50:14.450 --> 00:50:16.370
the light actually
penetrates into the eye

00:50:16.370 --> 00:50:18.600
and could damage the retina.

00:50:18.600 --> 00:50:20.690
And at longer
wavelengths, the energy

00:50:20.690 --> 00:50:22.370
is absorbed in the
outer part of the eye

00:50:22.370 --> 00:50:23.703
and might damage the outer part.

00:50:23.703 --> 00:50:26.060
So this is actually
the best frequency

00:50:26.060 --> 00:50:28.190
to use for eye safety reasons.

00:50:28.190 --> 00:50:30.830
And as you said, the
lasers have the advantage

00:50:30.830 --> 00:50:33.360
over the radars in the fact
that they have a very tight beam

00:50:33.360 --> 00:50:33.860
width.

00:50:33.860 --> 00:50:38.190
This unit has a beam width
of about 2/10 of a degree.

00:50:38.190 --> 00:50:40.520
So you can really focus
on a specific vehicle,

00:50:40.520 --> 00:50:44.679
whereas the radar had a very
wide beam width of 12 degrees.

00:50:44.679 --> 00:50:46.220
I would now like to
talk a little bit

00:50:46.220 --> 00:50:48.930
about the measurement
of acceleration.

00:50:48.930 --> 00:50:51.890
Now, in principle, we
can measure acceleration

00:50:51.890 --> 00:50:53.930
through the force it exerts.

00:50:53.930 --> 00:50:56.870
Since force is
equal to mass times

00:50:56.870 --> 00:50:59.810
acceleration, if we
have a known mass,

00:50:59.810 --> 00:51:04.280
we can, again, figure out
the acceleration from force.

00:51:04.280 --> 00:51:08.090
And most accelerometers
work on that principle.

00:51:08.090 --> 00:51:12.020
You can see with this
spring mass system, here,

00:51:12.020 --> 00:51:14.150
I have 100 gram mass.

00:51:14.150 --> 00:51:16.730
And the acceleration
due to gravity

00:51:16.730 --> 00:51:19.260
shows me 100 grams on the scale.

00:51:19.260 --> 00:51:22.190
If I were to increase the
acceleration-- if I were,

00:51:22.190 --> 00:51:27.950
basically, to accelerate the
system up or let it come down,

00:51:27.950 --> 00:51:29.570
let it drop--

00:51:29.570 --> 00:51:31.760
I would get a variation
in the force that

00:51:31.760 --> 00:51:34.380
was read on the scale, here.

00:51:34.380 --> 00:51:37.190
In fact, if I were to
drop the spring scale,

00:51:37.190 --> 00:51:44.170
the spring would return to
zero since I'm now in freefall.

00:51:44.170 --> 00:51:47.410
In fact, you can see that this
creates a complication for us,

00:51:47.410 --> 00:51:50.800
on the surface of the Earth, if
we want to measure acceleration

00:51:50.800 --> 00:51:55.240
for the purposes of figuring
out what the subsequent velocity

00:51:55.240 --> 00:51:56.380
would be.

00:51:56.380 --> 00:51:57.880
Because on the
surface of the Earth,

00:51:57.880 --> 00:52:01.390
even though this isn't moving,
it feels that 1g acceleration

00:52:01.390 --> 00:52:03.490
down.

00:52:03.490 --> 00:52:07.090
The acceleration we
feel on the Earth

00:52:07.090 --> 00:52:10.120
is a combination of the
gravitational acceleration

00:52:10.120 --> 00:52:13.160
and something that we call
the inertial acceleration.

00:52:13.160 --> 00:52:15.190
And in fact, that
inertial acceleration

00:52:15.190 --> 00:52:19.550
is what you would
integrate to get velocity.

00:52:19.550 --> 00:52:23.650
Now, like I say,
most accelerometers

00:52:23.650 --> 00:52:24.700
use this principle.

00:52:24.700 --> 00:52:27.790
However, most accelerometers
aren't this crude.

00:52:27.790 --> 00:52:34.180
I have, here, an example of a
piezoelectric accelerometer.

00:52:34.180 --> 00:52:37.450
And again, essentially,
what it has is the same sort

00:52:37.450 --> 00:52:38.870
of thing on the inside.

00:52:38.870 --> 00:52:42.970
It's got a small proof
mass or test mass inside.

00:52:42.970 --> 00:52:45.490
And it has a sensor.

00:52:45.490 --> 00:52:49.100
In this case it's a
piezoelectric sensor.

00:52:49.100 --> 00:52:55.510
Now that piezoelectric
sensor sends a voltage out

00:52:55.510 --> 00:52:57.370
to this amplifier.

00:52:57.370 --> 00:53:00.790
And then that
amplifier brings it up

00:53:00.790 --> 00:53:03.820
to a level which can be
detected on this oscilloscope.

00:53:03.820 --> 00:53:08.770
Now, if I move the
accelerometer up and down,

00:53:08.770 --> 00:53:11.320
you can see that the
acceleration signal shows up

00:53:11.320 --> 00:53:14.110
on the scope, here.

00:53:14.110 --> 00:53:17.380
Now, one problem with
piezoelectric accelerometers

00:53:17.380 --> 00:53:21.370
is that they really only
measure changes in acceleration.

00:53:21.370 --> 00:53:24.280
They're what we call AC devices.

00:53:24.280 --> 00:53:26.740
You need to use a different
type of accelerometer

00:53:26.740 --> 00:53:30.700
if you are really interested
in measuring the static, or DC,

00:53:30.700 --> 00:53:33.220
acceleration.

00:53:33.220 --> 00:53:36.040
Another thing to notice is that
this particular accelerometer

00:53:36.040 --> 00:53:38.620
is really only sensitive
in one direction.

00:53:38.620 --> 00:53:39.940
And that's very common.

00:53:39.940 --> 00:53:43.551
Right now, its direction of
sensitivity is up and down.

00:53:43.551 --> 00:53:44.050
Okay.

00:53:44.050 --> 00:53:45.790
So again, if I move
it up and down,

00:53:45.790 --> 00:53:47.630
I get a pretty good signal.

00:53:47.630 --> 00:53:49.780
If I move it left
and right, you can

00:53:49.780 --> 00:53:51.660
see that I don't get
too much of a signal.

00:53:51.660 --> 00:53:54.760
In fact, any signal you do
get is just due to the fact

00:53:54.760 --> 00:53:58.610
that I can't move it
left and right perfectly.

00:53:58.610 --> 00:54:03.190
Now, we talked a little bit
about integrating acceleration

00:54:03.190 --> 00:54:04.840
to get velocity.

00:54:04.840 --> 00:54:07.240
And in fact, there's
a navigation principle

00:54:07.240 --> 00:54:09.820
that's based on this.

00:54:09.820 --> 00:54:10.570
This is a model--

00:54:14.200 --> 00:54:17.590
actually, it's an old model--
of an inertial navigation unit.

00:54:17.590 --> 00:54:21.790
And in principle, if you
have three accelerometers,

00:54:21.790 --> 00:54:24.190
one measuring, say, north--

00:54:24.190 --> 00:54:27.700
the yellow things, here,
are the accelerometers--

00:54:27.700 --> 00:54:31.360
one measuring east,
and one measuring down,

00:54:31.360 --> 00:54:35.170
and you knew your starting
position and starting velocity,

00:54:35.170 --> 00:54:37.000
you could integrate
the acceleration

00:54:37.000 --> 00:54:41.890
to get velocity and integrate
the velocity to get position.

00:54:41.890 --> 00:54:46.690
So you would be able to navigate
without ever having to make

00:54:46.690 --> 00:54:48.670
any outside measurements.

00:54:48.670 --> 00:54:52.450
And this is very desirable for
applications such as submarines

00:54:52.450 --> 00:54:54.330
and some flight vehicles.

00:54:54.330 --> 00:54:57.880
And units like this are called
inertial navigation systems.

00:54:57.880 --> 00:55:02.380
Now, it isn't sufficient to
just measure the acceleration,

00:55:02.380 --> 00:55:05.570
because you also have to be
able to know which direction you

00:55:05.570 --> 00:55:06.620
were going.

00:55:06.620 --> 00:55:11.650
So these three red devices,
here, are gyroscopes.

00:55:11.650 --> 00:55:14.230
And what they do is they
measure any rotations

00:55:14.230 --> 00:55:17.830
that the vehicle has undergone.

00:55:17.830 --> 00:55:21.790
Essentially, in an older system,
such as this gimbal-based

00:55:21.790 --> 00:55:26.710
system, what would happen
is if the platform rotated--

00:55:26.710 --> 00:55:28.540
if the vehicle rotated--

00:55:28.540 --> 00:55:30.880
the gimbals would
adjust and keep

00:55:30.880 --> 00:55:35.410
the accelerometers oriented
north, east, and down.

00:55:35.410 --> 00:55:40.250
Nowadays, it's more common
to do that computationally.

00:55:40.250 --> 00:55:42.820
So basically, all
the sensors would

00:55:42.820 --> 00:55:47.620
be fixed in what we call a
strapdown inertial system.

00:55:47.620 --> 00:55:51.220
Now, one thing to think
about with inertial systems

00:55:51.220 --> 00:55:53.830
is that any errors
in the sensors,

00:55:53.830 --> 00:55:57.040
either the gyros or
the accelerometers,

00:55:57.040 --> 00:55:59.800
are integrated
actually two times.

00:55:59.800 --> 00:56:02.530
So an error in
acceleration would

00:56:02.530 --> 00:56:06.544
be integrated once, to result
in an error in velocity.

00:56:06.544 --> 00:56:07.960
And that velocity
error would then

00:56:07.960 --> 00:56:11.050
be integrated and result
in an error in position.

00:56:11.050 --> 00:56:14.900
So errors in inertial
systems tend to build.

00:56:14.900 --> 00:56:19.300
And it's necessary to
periodically update

00:56:19.300 --> 00:56:22.960
both the alignment and the
position of an inertial system

00:56:22.960 --> 00:56:25.680
to cancel out those errors.

00:56:25.680 --> 00:56:28.110
We've talked about
techniques for measuring

00:56:28.110 --> 00:56:31.110
distance, velocity, and
acceleration, ranging

00:56:31.110 --> 00:56:33.420
from simple
mechanical techniques

00:56:33.420 --> 00:56:37.150
to a variety of
time-of-flight techniques.

00:56:37.150 --> 00:56:39.550
Since distance, velocity,
and acceleration

00:56:39.550 --> 00:56:42.070
are related quantities,
we can often

00:56:42.070 --> 00:56:47.050
use a technique to measure
one to infer another.

00:56:47.050 --> 00:56:48.820
However, it's
important to remember

00:56:48.820 --> 00:56:50.890
that distance, velocity,
and acceleration

00:56:50.890 --> 00:56:52.804
are vector quantities.

00:56:52.804 --> 00:56:54.220
So it's important
to consider both

00:56:54.220 --> 00:56:59.080
the magnitude and direction
when making a measurement.

00:56:59.080 --> 00:57:02.130
